nd the adrenal cortex. As a result, individuals with adrenoleucodistrofia experience a range of neurological decline and various metabolic issues, including problems related to the adrenal glands.\n\nThe disorder is notable for its variable phenotypic expression, meaning that symptoms can differ significantly among affected individuals. It is relevant in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases due to its overlapping symptoms. Management of adrenoleucodistrofia often requires dietary interventions to help mitigate the effects of the disorder. Overall, adrenoleucodistrofia represents a complex interplay of genetic and metabolic factors that necessitate careful clinical attention.\",52\r\n10999,AGCML,\"AGCML is a biochemical marker associated with adrenoleukodystrophy, a genetic disorder that affects the adrenal glands and the nervous system. It is monitored during treatment to assess the condition of patients. AGCML refers specifically to very long-chain saturated fatty acids that accumulate in the bodies of individuals with adrenoleukodystrophy, leading to various health issues. These fatty acids can cause metabolic problems and are also linked to Reye-like syndrome, which further complicates the health of affected patients.\",3\r\n11011,C26:0,\"C26:0, also known as hexacosanoic acid, is a very long-chain saturated fatty acid that plays a significant role in the diagnosis of adrenoleucodystrophy (ALD). This fatty acid accumulates in patients suffering from ALD, and efforts are made to reduce its levels through dietary interventions.\",3\r\n10759,DRA. M.ª NURIA VIRGILI CASAS,Dra. M.ª Nuria Virgili Casas is a medical professional specializing in lipid disorders and their dietary management.,2\r\n8244,DIETARY RESTRICTIONS,\"Dietary restrictions are essential for managing the health of patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, primarily aimed at limiting the intake of very long-chain fatty acids. In addition to this specific restriction, dietary guidelines for these patients may also encompass a variety of other limitations, including gluten-free, low-purine, and vegetarian options. These comprehensive dietary restrictions are designed to support the overall well-being and nutritional needs of individuals affected by this condition.\",2\r\n11003,ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY,\"Adrenal insufficiency is a common complication in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, often requiring steroid treatment.\",1\r\n5042,GEN X-ALD,\"The GEN X-ALD gene is responsible for encoding a protein that functions as a peroxisomal membrane transporter, specifically involved in the transport of fatty acids within cells. Mutations in the X-ALD gene result in a condition known as adrenoleukodystrophy, which is characterized by the accumulation of very long-chain fatty acids due to impaired transport. This genetic alteration can lead to severe neurological and adrenal dysfunction, highlighting the critical role of the X-ALD gene in cellular fatty acid metabolism.\",1\r\n10763,DRA. ROSA BURGOS PELÁEZ,,1\r\n10986,DIETAS CONTROLADAS EN ÁCIDOS GRASOS,Controlled diets in fatty acids are dietary interventions aimed at managing conditions like adrenoleucodistrofia by regulating the intake of specific fatty acids.,1\r\n10987,PUBLIC HEALTH NUTR,Public Health Nutr is a peer-reviewed journal that focuses on research related to public health nutrition.,1\r\n10996,CORTEX ADRENAL,\"The adrenal cortex is the outer part of the adrenal glands, which are responsible for producing hormones, and is affected in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia.\",1\r\n10997,HEXA-COSANOIC ACID,\"Hexacosanoic acid (C26:0) is a very long-chain fatty acid that accumulates in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, contributing to the disease's pathology.\",1\r\n10998,TETRA-COSANOIC ACID,Tetracosanoic acid (C24:0) is another fatty acid that accumulates in lower amounts in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia.,1\r\n11000,FIBROBLASTS,Fibroblasts are cells that can be cultured to study the oxidation rates of A